      "order": true,
      "notes": [
        {
          "value": "The bubbles are hydrogen. Magnesium and zinc each took the place of the hydrogen in the acid, and each went into the liquid as its chloride: a single replacement, twice.",
          "sourceId": "chemguide-metals-dilute-acids"
        },
        {
          "value": "Copper did nothing. It stands below hydrogen in the order of the metals, and a metal below hydrogen does not push hydrogen out of an acid.",
          "sourceId": "chemguide-metals-dilute-acids",
          "alsoSourceId": "chemguide-reactivity-series"
        },
        {
          "value": "The higher a metal stands in that order, the faster it works on an acid. So the three run, most active first: magnesium, zinc, copper.",
          "sourceId": "chemguide-metals-dilute-acids",
